{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "The nature/profile of IT incidents — characterizing each ticket by what failed, how it manifested, its scope, and resolution/timing context, effectively decomposing incidents into analyzable failure dimensions.",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "affected_system",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Identifies the technical component involved — a core facet of what an incident is about and a strong analytical predictor."
    },
    {
      "name": "symptom_type",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Characterizes the failure mode; a coherent facet describing how the incident manifests."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_scope",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Captures blast radius (individual vs infrastructure-wide), a meaningful severity/impact facet."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_open_unresolved",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Resolution-state facet of the incident lifecycle; actionable for backlog analysis."
    },
    {
      "name": "self_resolved",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Resolution-path facet distinguishing auto/self-resolution from intervention."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Flags recurring incidents — a genuinely useful analytical dimension, though its extreme skew (472/28) raises reliability concerns."
    },
    {
      "name": "opened_hour_of_day",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Temporal context facet useful for staffing/pattern analysis; derivable from opened_at."
    },
    {
      "name": "access_context",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Remote vs on-premise context; relevant facet though largely 'unspecified' (378), limiting its value."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.85,
  "structure_quality": 0.8,
  "focus_actionability": 0.85,
  "rationale": "The columns collectively centre on one coherent inferable focus: profiling incident tickets by their failure characteristics and lifecycle context. They span differentiated, non-redundant facets — what failed (affected_system), how (symptom_type), scale (incident_scope), resolution (is_open_unresolved, self_resolved), recurrence, and temporal/access context — rather than a scattered grab-bag or mechanical text metrics. This is a sound decomposition at a useful granularity and directly actionable (an analyst can pivot on affected_system × symptom_type × scope to find hotspots). Deductions: recurrence_signal and access_context show very skewed/mostly-unspecified distributions suggesting weak inference reliability, and self_resolved partly overlaps with is_open_unresolved, slightly diluting structural cleanliness."
}

